A||||24||Joe4x4||Wrap it or not ?||||1||
Z||000000||Joe4x4||05-24-2001||06:23 AM||||Hmmmm was thinking , what about header wrap<BR>on the exhust to keep the engine bay area<BR>cooler? Keep the heat in the exhust pipes.<P>I think that there is something about the wrap, Pros and Cons?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Need For Speed........<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/car1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||KRMFiero||05-24-2001||06:57 AM||||I cant see any cons, infact i have some here i bought from Jegs that is going to go on my 4.5 before it goes in<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hartz||05-24-2001||09:30 AM||||If you do, just realize that the wrapped area will collect moisture and therefore wear out quicker than normal.  I have a set of headers on my V8 car that are wrapped, and they are starting to rust out and leak after only 4000 miles!  If you have the funds, ceramic coating is a much better option.<P>Just my $.02.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||13
Z||000003||theogre||05-24-2001||10:56 AM||||I personally don't like wrap. There are various tricks people use to make it look better and repel dirt but it's still pretty nasty stuff. A big issue... If you don't install it evenly it can cause cracked parts.<P>I'd rather coat them. You can send them to <A HREF="http://www.jet-hot.com/" TARGET=_blank>Jet Hot</A> or get stuff and do it yourself from  <A HREF="http://www.techlinecoatings.com/" TARGET=_blank>Tech Line Coatings</A> <P>The coating <I>may</I> not be as effective(I've never seen a direct performance comparison) as a wrap, but it won't cause clearance problems etc. If you send them out you can have the inside coated as well making the stuff even more effective.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||05-24-2001||12:42 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B><P>I'd rather coat them. You can send them to <A HREF="http://www.jet-hot.com/" TARGET=_blank>Jet Hot</A> or get stuff and do it yourself from  <A HREF="http://www.techlinecoatings.com/" TARGET=_blank>Tech Line Coatings</A> <P>The coating <I>may</I> not be as effective(I've never seen a direct performance comparison) as a wrap, but it won't cause clearance problems etc. If you send them out you can have the inside coated as well making the stuff even more effective.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ogre<BR>Much like Aus and Orief, You never fail to amaize me with your technical prowess and the abundance of information you provide at an instant   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>Thanks <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||05-24-2001||03:52 PM||||I've heard that some header companies will void the warranty if you use it because the extra heat tends to warp them. If it's actually true, I don't know.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||05-24-2001||07:02 PM||||Though I have never tried them, It seems to me that wrapping your headers will definatly weaken the metal from all that extra heat.  Its almost like how people who run in rubber suits to lose weight tend to pass out, our bodies arent made to have all that extra heat.<P>Maybe I'm wrong, but that's just my opinion.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000007||KissMySSFiero||05-24-2001||07:47 PM||||Jet hot is the way to go.  my uncle just got a set done for his truck.  if you give them a set of new headers they have a lifetime warrenty against rust thru and flaking.  not to mention they look so good. he paid 179 for a set of shorty headers.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||05-24-2001||08:17 PM||||I used the wrap on my Civic for over 50,000 miles with no problems. When the car got wrecked, the impact broke the lower flange and I put the stock manifold back on. I pulled the wrap off the header and it was in pretty good shape. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tina||05-24-2001||08:50 PM||||Can only agree with Hartz and theogre.<BR>Plus it voids most warranties on headers.<P>Tina||||reg||1
Z||000010||cadaver||05-24-2001||11:21 PM||||NEVER WRAP YOUR PIPES. It WILL trash them. It causes hot spots and moisture retention. jethot or ceramic is the way to go. I know. "The race cars have there headers wrapped". They also rebuild the motor before the next race and just install new pipes when needed.<BR>Cooter that because it never got hot enough to do anything anyway. Sorry. Just funnin.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BV MotorSports||05-25-2001||01:32 AM||||I have used thermo tec heat wrap on a few fieros and on my conquest.<BR>Remember the exhaust on the fiero is stainless steel...moisture and corrosion is a non-issue. I wouldnt use it on the exhaust manifolds cause they tend to develop metal fatigue and crack but the Y-pipe is worth wrapping.<P>I have my turbo, down pipe and OVC pipe on the 'Quest wrapped and it makes a HUGE difference on a turbocharged car.<P>Steven<BR><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 93mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000012||c m west||05-25-2001||11:16 AM||||Hartz - how the hell can a tube that shortly reaches 1000 degrees be affected in the slightest by moisture?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Hartz||05-25-2001||12:45 PM||||I don't pretend to understand the physics of it all!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I would guess it is from the giant vents above the engine that allow rain to seep in and get under the wrap, but who knows.  I have heard more people come out against wrap than for it.  If it works for some people - that's cool.  Myself, I will be sticking with ceramic coating in the future.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000014||84Bill||05-25-2001||12:49 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>Hartz - how the hell can a tube that shortly reaches 1000 degrees be affected in the slightest by moisture?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uhh well when the moon and stars align in the right apogee while the engine, being cool for some time, allowing the otherwise warm damp air to cantact said headers causing condensation thus resulting in oxidation of the aformentioned header &lt;big breath&gt; the wrap acting as a spongal material retains moisture from the above explaind causing further deterioation of the material around witch it is wraped. <P>I just could not help myself   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-25-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000015||84Bill||05-25-2001||01:01 PM||||hate it when I do that!<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Hartz||05-25-2001||01:01 PM||||See - I TOLD you I didn't understand the physics of it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000017||rockcrawl||05-25-2001||01:24 PM||||Why not coat 'em <B>and</B> wrap 'em? That's what I did. The wrap does retain heat in the pipes, I have (had) proof. I had a set of custom headers coated with aluminum, real aluminum, not some aerosol crap. Only for rust prevention. I ran them for a few months with no problems other than extreme high underhood temps that were destroting everything that heat destroys. I wrapped the pipes with Thermotec. No noticeable change in underhood temp, but the aluminum coating on the pipes melted and actually pushed through the fiberglass weave! Kinda scary. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Formula88||07-14-2001||05:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>Hartz - how the hell can a tube that shortly reaches 1000 degrees be affected in the slightest by moisture?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe because when you car is stitting outside in the rain, with the engine off, and the water is soaking into the wrap - the pipes are, what, ambient temperature?  Let's be generous and say summertime in the desert, 120-140 degrees?  It's only a 1000 degrees when running, but corrosion can take place even when the engine's off.<P>Personally, I would prefer coating, but I'm sure some people have had good luck with wraps.  I just wouldn't want to use it.||||reg||6
Z||000019||88formula||07-14-2001||05:56 PM||||c m west, It will rust out when it's raining out and your car is sitting in the driveway not running like while you sleep.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Wolfhound||07-14-2001||06:47 PM||||Rust Never Sleeps!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000021||maryjane||07-14-2001||08:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wolfhound:<BR><B>Rust Never Sleeps!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>It's kinda like this forum then. Does anyone here EVER sleep?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Will||07-14-2001||09:30 PM||||My take on wrap and coating is that they are usually used in the wrong place. Putting wrap or coating on the outside of a header or pipe causes the metal itself to get very hot because it can not radiate its heat. This causes thermal cycle stresses which can eventually crack your pipes.<BR>Coat only the inside of pipes or headers. The coating on the inside is a thermal barrier between the exhaust and the pipe, but the pipe can still radiate away what heat it does absorb from the exhaust and doesn't get as hot as it would if it were wrapped or coated on the outside.<BR>As West Coast Fiero said, his headers got hot enough under the wraps to melt aluminum.||||reg||1
Z||000023||fotofrank||07-14-2001||09:59 PM||||I had wrap on the header of my "84 Duke for about 2&1/2 years. The header was stock and kept the temp down in the engine comartment. I recently installed a Hooker header on that car and the original stock header is a OK. No cracks or obveous stress or strain areas. I have not put wrap on the Hooker header but am thinking about it. The under hood(deck led)temp is not as high as with the un-wraped stock header was. NASCAR,CART, IRL don't wrap headers 'cause they spend money on GOOD coatings that work better than wrap.<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4 <A HREF="http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fotofrank (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000024||theogre||07-14-2001||10:44 PM||||Good coatings.... Jet Hot isn't cheap. They may be backed up. I beleive they lost one of their 3 facilities in a fire in Bridgeport PA. I beleive that as long as you send them a new part they warantee against rust thru. Jet Hot is well proven product.<P>Tech Line has DIY materials. They aren't exactly cheap either. Problem with their products is you can't reliably coat the inside of things like Jet Hot can. You may be able to send parts ot one of the shops that apply Tech Line profesional products for interior treatment. Probably costs the same as Jet Hot to do that.<P>Before wrapping the new header, check the warantee info. Wrap can void the wrantee. ||||reg||1
